   "format": "short_answer",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Consider the curve y^2 = x^3 + x^2, for which dy/dx = (3x^2 + 2x)/(2y). Find the x-coordinate of the point on the curve at which the tangent line is vertical."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Negate",
         1
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "A vertical tangent needs the denominator to be zero, so y = 0.",
         "mathjson": 0
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Substitute y = 0 into the curve: x^3 + x^2 = x^2 (x + 1) = 0, so the candidates are (0, 0) and (-1, 0).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Multiply",
            [
               "Power",
               "x",
               2
            ],
            [
               "Add",
               "x",
               1
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "At (0, 0) the numerator 3x^2 + 2x is also 0, so dy/dx has the form 0/0 and the condition for a vertical tangent is not met there. At (-1, 0) the numerator is 3 - 2 = 1, which is nonzero.",
         "mathjson": 1
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "The tangent line is vertical only at (-1, 0), so the x-coordinate is -1.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Negate",
            1
         ]
      }
   ],
